Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: adapted from the book by
Author: Margery Williams

==

FADE IN:

EXT./INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A child's stocking. Wedged at the top, a VELVETEEN RABBIT -- fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white, thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

THE BOY loves him among nuts, oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, a clockwork mouse.

Tissue paper erupts. Aunts and Uncles, parcels, noise.

The Velveteen Rabbit sits overlooked on the nursery floor.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Toy cupboard. Mechanical toys swagger. A model boat. Timothy, a jointed wooden lion, puts on airs.

The Rabbit -- shy, insignificant.

Only the SKIN HORSE is kind: brown coat bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs sparse.

INT. NURSERY - BY THE FENDER - DAY

Rabbit and Skin Horse side by side near the fender, before Nana tidies.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs -- longing, and afraid of shabbiness.

INT. NURSERY - BEDTIME

The Boy cannot find the china dog. NANA, in a hurry, sees the toy cupboard open and swoops.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

Tight hugs. Rolled on. Pushed under the pillow.

Then whispers. Nice tunnels under the bedclothes -- like burrows. Night-light burning on the mantelpiece. The Rabbit snuggles under the Boy's chin; the Boy's hands clasped round him.

MONTAGE -- TIME PASSES

Velveteen fur shabbier. Tail unsewn. Pink rubbed off the nose where the Boy has kissed him.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

Wheelbarrow rides. Picnics on the grass. Fairy huts under the raspberry canes. Burrows in the flower bed.

Dusk. The Rabbit left on the lawn, wet with dew, earthy. Nana searches with a candle.

INT. NURSERY / BOY'S BED - NIGHT

Nana rubs him with a corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy sits up, hands out.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The little Rabbit hears. Boot-button eyes hold a look of wisdom and beauty.

EXT. WOOD - JUNE EVENING

The Boy makes the Rabbit a nest in the bracken and wanders off.

Ants run between velvet paws.

Two WILD RABBITS creep from tall bracken -- furry, seams invisible, shape changing as they move. Noses twitch. They come close.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
It's as easy as anything.

A big hop sideways; stands on hind legs.

WILD RABBIT (CONT'D)
I don't believe you can!

RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit has no hind legs -- the back all one piece like a pincushion. He sits still.

RABBIT
I don't want to!

The wild rabbit stretches his neck.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

He whirls and dances. The little Rabbit grows dizzy.

RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The strange rabbit comes so close whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear -- then wrinkles his nose, flattens ears, jumps backwards.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past. Stamp of feet; flash of white tails -- the wild rabbits gone.

RABBIT (CONT'D)
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Ants. Bracken sway. Little white moths. The Boy carries him home.

INT. BOY'S BED - ILLNESS

The Boy's face flushed. He talks in his sleep. His body so hot it burns the Rabbit.

Strange people come and go. A light burns all night.

The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring -- afraid someone might take him away.

He whispers planned garden games into the Boy's ear.

The fever turns. Picture-books. The Rabbit at his side.

INT. NURSERY - BRIGHT MORNING

Windows wide open. The Boy carried to the balcony, wrapped in a shawl.

The Rabbit tangled in bedclothes, head peeping, listening.

Talk of the seaside tomorrow. The room to be disinfected. Books and toys played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana catches sight of him.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! -- Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - DAY TO NIGHT

The little Rabbit put into a sack with old picture-books and rubbish, carried to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house. Bonfire place. The gardener is busy -- tomorrow morning.

NIGHT.

Elsewhere, the Boy sleeps with a new white plush bunny -- and dreams of the seaside.

In the sack, untied, the Rabbit wriggles his head free. Thin threadbare coat. Shivering.

Raspberry canes in shadow. Memories of sunlit hours, fairy huts, the wood, the ants, the day he knew he was Real, the Skin Horse.

A tear -- a real tear -- trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where the tear fell, a flower grows: slender green leaves the colour of emeralds, a blossom like a golden cup.

The blossom opens. Out steps a FAIRY -- dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers round her neck and in her hair.

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

He looks -- her face almost remembered.

FAIRY (CONT'D)
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and flies with him into the wood.

EXT. WOOD - MOONLIT GLADE

Moon risen. Bracken fronds like frosted silver. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass -- then stop, ringed, staring.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

FAIRY (CONT'D)
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits still -- remembering hind legs, ashamed of being one piece.

Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch.

He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny. Ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers brush the grass.

One leap -- then springing, jumping sideways, whirling. Joy.

When he looks for the Fairy, she is gone.

He is a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY (LATER)

Autumn and winter have passed. Warm sunny spring.

The Boy plays in the wood behind the house.

Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep. One brown all over. The other -- strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted. Soft nose. Round black eyes. Something familiar.

On the Boy's face, a quiet thought: he looks just like the old Bunny lost when scarlet fever came.

But the Boy never knows it really is his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.

THE END
